// spec: at compile time an `if` declaring its condition (`if (const char* name = find(k))`) or with an init statement (`if (int n = f(); n > 2)`) runs as at run time, the variables living through both branches and destroyed after (`2 0 3 1`)
// found while making an enum's appendTo (design.md 14) run at compile time: the library's `if (const char* name = kppEnumeratorName(value))` was no constant
extern "C" import "stdio.h";
constexpr const char* find(int k) { return k == 1 ? "one" : nullptr; }
constexpr int named(int k)
{
  if (const char* name = find(k))
    return name[0] == 'o' ? 2 : 1;
  else
    return name == nullptr ? 0 : 9;
}
constexpr int initialized(int k)
{
  if (String s = "abc"; k > 0)
    return int(s.size());
  return 1;
}
static_assert(named(1) == 2 && named(2) == 0);
static_assert(initialized(1) == 3 && initialized(0) == 1);
constexpr int a = named(1), b = named(2), c = initialized(1), d = initialized(0);
int main()
{
  printf("%d %d %d %d\n", a, b, c, d);
  return 0;
}
